.claude/worktrees/

# NO GAME LOG IS TRACKED. Every log records a real child playing: the typing log
# carries their name alongside their per-keystroke mistakes, and the math and
# spelling logs carry a timestamped record of exactly what they could not do.
# This repo has an origin, and none of that should follow it there.
#
# It is also the practical thing. The logs are the largest and fastest-growing
# files in the repo and nothing reads them across machines, so tracking them
# means cloning a stranger's child's history to play a game. Clone the code, play
# on your own machine, build your own log.
#
# This reverses math spec §14, which committed data/math-log.jsonl deliberately
# so the derivation could be checked against real data. That was right while the
# game was a spike and one person had the repo. It stops being right the moment
# a third game arrives and the repo is meant to be cloned.
#
# The file remains in the history of commits before 2026-08-02. Untracking it now
# stops it growing; it does not erase it.
data/*.jsonl

# API keys. This repo has a public origin, so a key committed here is a key
# published — and a leaked key is not recoverable by deleting the file, because
# the commit keeps it. Nothing in the repo reads a key except tools/fetch-words.js,
# and it reads it from the environment only.
#
# The negation matters: .env.example is the tracked template and carries no key.
# It exists so a fresh clone can see WHICH variables to set without anyone having
# to remember. Keep every real value out of it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Merriam-Webster's terms forbid duplicating or redistributing their data, and
# committing a cache to a repo with a public origin is redistribution. Each
# clone brings its own MW_KEY and builds its own cache via tools/fetch-words.js.
# The game is fully playable with neither — audio falls back to local TTS.
data/audio/
data/words/
